# Generic concepts — naming and usage

When a idea is **not unique to one product area**, model and implement it as a **generic concept** in shared code (`lib/`, `components/ui/`, cross-feature hooks). Feature modules **compose** generic building blocks; they should not re-own the same idea under a domain prefix or parallel implementation.

Applies to **any** reusable concept — data shapes, algorithms, UI patterns, state machines, side-effect flows — not only geometry or canvas code.

Domain context belongs in the **module path**, **call site**, and **thin adapters** — not in duplicated logic or prefixed primitives.

## Naming

- Name after the **concept**: `Rectangle`, `PaginatedList`, `ModalCoordinator`, `ServiceOutcome` — not `CompositionRectangle`, `StoryListPage`, `CharacterModalHelper`.
- Use domain-prefixed names only for genuine product ownership (`StoryChapter`, `StoryChatOrchestrator`), OpenAPI DTOs, or local disambiguation when splitting modules is impractical.

## Usage

- **Search shared code first.** Before adding feature-local helpers, check `lib/`, `components/ui/`, the UI catalog, and existing cross-feature utils for the same concept.
- **Adapt at the boundary; run generic logic in the middle.** Map domain types to generic shapes once, then call shared functions — do not reimplement the behavior in domain terms.

```typescript
// ❌ Same concept, reimplemented per feature
function parseStoryBrowseResponse(raw) { /* custom page/count extraction */ }
function parseTemplateBrowseResponse(raw) { /* duplicate pagination rules */ }

// ✅ Generic concept + thin mappers at the HTTP edge
const outcome = normalizePaginatedList(raw, mapStorySummary)
```

- **Compose, don't fork.** Pagination, errors, modals, empty/loading states, selection, undo history, validation, and filtering should each have **one** generic implementation. Feature code wires inputs, interprets outputs, and applies product-specific side effects (routes, copy, API resource).
- **Extract upward when reuse appears.** If logic is not owned by a single feature, move it to shared code and leave a small mapper — do not copy-paste with renamed types or parallel `feature*` utilities.
- **One concept, one code path.** Avoid parallel domain-specific copies of the same idea; if behavior diverges, extend the generic API (parameter, strategy, variant) rather than duplicating.

## When domain-specific is OK

- Product rules that only make sense in one feature (story chat scroll policy, template browse filters).
- Wire/API shapes — consume OpenAPI types as published.
- Adapters that translate **different** domain models into a shared generic model (that is the intended pattern).

## While editing

- Ask: *Is this a product entity/rule, or a reusable concept?* Product → domain name and feature home. Concept → generic name and shared home.
- Ask: *Would another feature use this unchanged?* If yes → generic name, generic module, generic call path.
- When touching duplicated domain-prefixed utils, prefer consolidating into the existing generic helper instead of extending the duplicate.

